---
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const technologies = [
  "Home Assistant",
  "ESP32",
  "ESPHome",
  "Zigbee",
  "Frigate",
  "RTSP",
  "MQTT",
  "YAML",
];

const automations = [
  {
    title: "Vehicle Arrival",
    description:
      "Uses phone-based GPS presence to determine when the vehicle arrives home and trigger garage and other related automations.",
    image: `${base}/projects/home-assistant/vehicle-arrival.png`,
  },
  {
    title: "Stair Light",
    description:
      "Stairs lighting controlled soely through motion detetcion and sensor data.",
    image: `${base}/projects/home-assistant/frigate.png`,
  },
  {
    title: "Smart Blinds",
    description:
      "Smart blinds are integrated into Home Assistant for centralized control and daily automation.",
    image: `${base}/projects/home-assistant/blinds.png`,
  },
  {
    title: "ESP32 Control Panel",
    description:
      "A custom ESP32-S3 touchscreen interface provides a dedicated dashboard for controlling my bedroom.",
    image: `${base}/projects/home-assistant/control-panel.png`,
  },
];
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />

    <title>Home Assistant Smart Home | Taninder Thiara</title>

    <meta
      name="description"
      content="A technical case study of Taninder Thiara's locally controlled Home Assistant smart-home ecosystem integrating ESP32, ESPHome, Zigbee, Frigate and custom automations."
    />

    <link
      rel="icon"
      type="image/png"
      href={`${base}/favicon.png`}
    />
  </head>

  <body class="bg-[var(--background)] text-[var(--white)] sm:px-28 lg:px-20 px-9">

    <!-- Navigation -->
    <nav class="max-w-5xl mx-auto py-8">
      <a
        href={`${base}/`}
        class="text-[var(--white-icon)] hover:text-white transition"
      >
        ← Back to Portfolio
      </a>
    </nav>


    <main class="max-w-5xl mx-auto pb-4">

      <!-- Hero -->
      <section class="pt-12 md:pt-4">

        <p class="text-base md:text-lg uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)] mb-5 shiny-sec">
          Featured Project · IoT / Automation
        </p>

        <h1 class="text-5xl md:text-7xl font-semibold leading-[1.05] mb-8">
          Home Assistant
          <br />
          Smart Home
        </h1>

        <p class="max-w-3xl text-lg md:text-xl text-[var(--white-icon)] leading-relaxed">
          A locally controlled smart-home ecosystem built around Home Assistant,
          connecting IoT devices, custom ESP32 hardware, Zigbee devices,
          security cameras and vehicle presence into a single automation platform.
        </p>


        <!-- Technology tags -->
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mt-4">
          {
            technologies.map((technology) => (
              <span
                class="px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)]"
              >
                {technology}
              </span>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>
      <!-- Hero image -->
      <section class="mt-4">

        <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)]">
          <img
            src={`${base}/projects/home-assistant/home-assistant.png`}
            alt="Home Assistant smart home dashboard"
            class="w-full object-cover"
          />
        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- Overview -->
      <section class="mt-8">

        <div class="mb-8">
          <p class="text-base md:text-lg uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)] mb-4 shiny-sec">
            Overview
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-medium leading-tight">
            Building a connected home
          </h2>
        </div>

        <div class="max-w-4xl text-[var(--white-icon)] text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-6">

          <p>
            This project started as an effort to bring different smart-home
            devices into one locally controlled system rather than relying on
            separate manufacturer applications.
          </p>

          <p>
            Home Assistant acts as the central platform, connecting devices
            through Zigbee, Wi-Fi, ESPHome and other local integrations.
            Custom automations then allow these devices to respond to events
            such as vehicle arrival, time of day, device state and camera
            detections.
          </p>

          <p>
            The system has also evolved beyond basic device control, with
            custom ESP32 hardware, camera processing through Frigate and
            location-based automations.
          </p>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- Architecture -->
      <section class="mt-4">

        <div class="mb-4">
          <p class="text-base md:text-lg uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)] mb-4 shiny-sec">
            Architecture
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-medium leading-tight">
            How everything connects
          </h2>
        </div>


        <!-- Architecture layout -->
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-10 lg:gap-10 items-center">

          <!-- Diagram -->
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] p-6 md:p-6">

            <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-3">

              <!-- Devices -->
              <div class="w-full max-w-xs rounded-xl border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] p-5 text-center">

                <p class="text-xl font-semibold">
                  Devices
                </p>

                <p class="text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
                  Zigbee · ESP32 · Cameras · Sensors
                </p>

              </div>


              <!-- Arrow -->
              <div class="text-2xl text-[var(--sec)]">
                ↓
              </div>


              <!-- Home Assistant -->
              <div class="w-full max-w-xs rounded-xl border border-[var(--sec)] bg-[#a476ff10] p-5 text-center">

                <p class="text-xl font-semibold">
                  Home Assistant
                </p>

                <p class="text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
                  Integrations · States · Automations
                </p>

              </div>


              <!-- Arrow -->
              <div class="text-2xl text-[var(--sec)]">
                ↓
              </div>


              <!-- Actions -->
              <div class="w-full max-w-xs rounded-xl border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] p-5 text-center">

                <p class="text-xl font-semibold">
                  Actions
                </p>

                <p class="text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
                  Lights · Blinds · Garage · Notifications
                </p>

              </div>

            </div>

          </div>


          <!-- Explanation -->
          <div class="text-[var(--white-icon)] text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-6">

            <p>
              The system is built around Home Assistant as the central
              automation and orchestration layer.
            </p>

            <p>
              Devices communicate with Home Assistant through different
              protocols and integrations. Zigbee devices, ESP32 hardware,
              cameras and other sensors provide the inputs that Home Assistant
              can monitor and process.
            </p>

            <p>
              Home Assistant then evaluates those states and events against
              configured automations before triggering an action. This keeps
              the individual devices decoupled from the logic controlling
              them.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- Automations -->
      <section class="mt-8">

        <div class="mb-10">
          <p class="text-base md:text-lg uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)] mb-4 shiny-sec">
            Automations
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-medium leading-tight mb-5">
            Turning events into actions
          </h2>

          <p class="max-w-3xl text-[var(--white-icon)] text-lg leading-relaxed">
            Instead of controlling every device manually, Home Assistant
            combines information from multiple sources to trigger actions
            throughout the home. 
            <br>
            Different brands can be synced together to perform actions that otherwise would not be possible!
          </p>
        </div>


        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">

          {
            automations.map((automation) => (
              <article class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] overflow-hidden">

                <img
                  src={automation.image}
                  alt={automation.title}
                  class="w-full aspect-video object-cover"
                  loading="lazy"
                />

                <div class="p-4">

                  <h3 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-semibold mb-3">
                    {automation.title}
                  </h3>

                  <p class="text-[var(--white-icon)] leading-relaxed">
                    {automation.description}
                  </p>

                </div>

              </article>
            ))
          }

        </div>

      </section>

          <!-- Smart blinds -->
      <section class="mt-10">
        <div class="mb-10">
          <p
            class="text-base md:text-lg uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)] mb-4 shiny-sec"
          >
            Smart Blinds
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-medium leading-tight">
            From 15 manual blinds to full automation
          </h2>
        </div>

        <div class="max-w-4xl text-[var(--white-icon)] text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-6">
          <p>
            Opening and closing nearly 15 blinds every day became a surprisingly
            tedious routine. Rather than continuing to operate each blind
            manually, I decided to build a system that could automate the
            entire process.
          </p>

          <p>
            The project started with selecting the right hardware. I took
            measurements for each window, researched different smart-blind
            technologies and evaluated how the blinds would communicate with
            the rest of the smart home. Because the blinds are battery powered,
            power consumption was an important consideration. I wanted to avoid
            putting unnecessary load on the home's Wi-Fi network while also
            maximizing battery life.
          </p>

          <p>
            After researching the available options, I chose rechargeable
            Zigbee blinds. Zigbee provided a lower-power wireless option that
            could integrate with the existing smart-home infrastructure without
            putting every blind directly onto Wi-Fi.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Hardware challenge -->
        <div class="mt-12 grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-10 lg:gap-16 items-center">
          <div
            class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] p-7 md:p-9"
          >
            <p
              class="text-base uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)] mb-4 shiny-sec"
            >
              Challenge
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-semibold mb-5">
              They weren't actually Zigbee blinds
            </h3>

            <p class="text-[var(--white-icon)] leading-relaxed">
              The blinds were marketed as Zigbee-compatible, but after
              integrating them into the system I discovered that the blinds
              themselves were actually controlled using RF. The Zigbee hub
              acted as a bridge between the blinds and the Tuya ecosystem.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="text-[var(--white-icon)] text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-6">
            <p>
              This introduced a reliability problem. The hub would occasionally
              go offline, and individual blinds could disconnect from the
              system. That meant an automation could run successfully while
              some blinds failed to respond.
            </p>

            <p>
              Instead of replacing the blinds, I decided to remove the
              unreliable dependency and communicate with the RF-controlled
              blinds directly.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ESP32 solution -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <div class="mb-10">
            <p
              class="text-base md:text-lg uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)] mb-4 shiny-sec"
            >
              Engineering Solution
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-medium leading-tight">
              Replacing the unreliable hub with an ESP32 RF controller
            </h3>
          </div>

          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-10 lg:gap-16 items-center">
            <div
              class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] p-7 md:p-9"
            >
              <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-4">
                <div
                  class="w-full rounded-xl border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] p-5 text-center"
                >
                  <p class="text-xl font-semibold">Home Assistant</p>
                  <p class="text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
                    Automation &amp; Scheduling
                  </p>
                </div>

                <div class="text-2xl text-[var(--sec)]">
                  ↓
                </div>

                <div
                  class="w-full rounded-xl border border-[var(--sec)] bg-[#a476ff10] p-5 text-center"
                >
                  <p class="text-xl font-semibold">ESP32</p>
                  <p class="text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
                    Custom RF control
                  </p>
                </div>

                <div class="text-2xl text-[var(--sec)]">
                  ↓
                </div>

                <div
                  class="w-full rounded-xl border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] p-5 text-center"
                >
                  <p class="text-xl font-semibold">RF Blinds</p>
                  <p class="text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
                    Open · Close · Stop
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="text-[var(--white-icon)] text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-6">
              <p>
                I used an ESP32 with an RF transceiver to capture and reproduce
                the wireless commands used by the blinds. Rather than relying
                on the Tuya Zigbee hub, the ESP32 became the direct bridge
                between Home Assistant and the RF-controlled blinds.
              </p>

              <p>
                The RF commands were reverse engineered by capturing the
                signals generated by the original controller and then
                reproducing those commands from the ESP32. I then integrated
                the custom controller with Home Assistant so the blinds could
                be exposed to the same automation system as the rest of the
                house.
              </p>

              <p>
                This approach eliminated the unreliable hub from the control
                path while giving me direct control over the blinds through
                software and custom hardware.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Final automation -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <div class="mb-10">
            <p
              class="text-base md:text-lg uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)] mb-4 shiny-sec"
            >
              Final Result
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-medium leading-tight">
              A completely automated daily routine
            </h3>
          </div>

          <div class="max-w-4xl text-[var(--white-icon)] text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-6">
            <p>
              The finished system now handles the daily opening and closing of
              the blinds automatically. Home Assistant uses sunrise and sunset
              as triggers, sending the appropriate commands to the ESP32 RF
              controller.
            </p>

            <p>
              What started as a repetitive task involving nearly 15 individual
              blinds is now handled automatically without requiring manual
              interaction. The project also demonstrated how custom hardware
              and software can be used to work around limitations in
              off-the-shelf smart-home products.
            </p>

            <p>
              I also now use use the smart blinds in other automations such as the one in the bathroom
              is connected to a door sensor. Once someone enters to use the restroom and closes the door,
              the blind closes all the way for privacy. Once done, and the door is opened, the blinds open
              to the set amount. <br>
              Also, I have an automation setup for the ones in my room to open acording to my daily alarm.
              The options really are endless!
            </p>
          </div>

          <div
            class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-[var(--sec)] bg-[#a476ff10] p-7 md:p-9"
          >
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-8 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold">~15</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
                  Blinds automated
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold">ESP32</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
                  Custom RF controller
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold">2× Daily</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-[var(--white-icon)] mt-2">
                  Sunrise &amp; sunset automation + more
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Vehicle automation -->
      <section class="mt-8">

        <div class="mb-10">
          <p class="text-base md:text-lg uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)] mb-4 shiny-sec">
            Example Automation
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-medium leading-tight">
            Vehicle arrival detection
          </h2>
        </div>

        <p class="max-w-3xl text-[var(--white-icon)] text-lg leading-relaxed mb-10">
          One of the more complex automations uses location data from a
          mobile device to determine when the vehicle is approaching home.
          Home Assistant can then use that presence information as an input
          to other automations.
        </p>


        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] bg-[#1414149c] p-6 md:p-10">

          <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row items-center justify-between gap-6">

            <div class="text-center">
              <div class="text-3xl mb-2">📱</div>
              <p class="font-medium">Phone GPS</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-[var(--white-icon)]">
                Location
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="text-2xl text-[var(--sec)]">
              →
            </div>

            <div class="text-center">
              <div class="text-3xl mb-2">📍</div>
              <p class="font-medium">Enter Home Zone</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-[var(--white-icon)]">
                Geofence, Presence
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="text-2xl text-[var(--sec)]">
              →
            </div>

            <div class="text-center">
              <div class="text-3xl mb-2">⚙️</div>
              <p class="font-medium">Automation</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-[var(--white-icon)]">
                Logic
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="text-2xl text-[var(--sec)]">
              →
            </div>

            <div class="text-center">
              <div class="text-3xl mb-2">🏠</div>
              <p class="font-medium">Home Action</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-[var(--white-icon)]">
                Open Garage, Notification 
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- Camera system -->
      <section class="mt-8">

        <div class="mb-10">
          <p class="text-base md:text-lg uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)] mb-4 shiny-sec">
            Computer Vision
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-medium leading-tight">
            Local camera processing with Frigate
          </h2>
        </div>


        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-10 lg:gap-16 items-center">

          <div class="text-[var(--white-icon)] text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-6">

            <p>
              Security cameras provide RTSP streams that can be processed
              locally through Frigate rather than sending the camera feeds
              to an external cloud service.
            </p>

            <p>
              Frigate provides object detection and integrates with
              Home Assistant, allowing camera events to become inputs for
              automations and notifications.
            </p>

          </div>


          <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)]">
            <img
              src={`${base}/projects/home-assistant/frigate-detail.png`}
              alt="Frigate camera detection"
              class="w-full object-cover"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- ESP32 -->
      <section class="mt-8">

        <div class="mb-10">
          <p class="text-base md:text-lg uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)] mb-4 shiny-sec">
            Custom Hardware
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-medium leading-tight">
            ESP32 and ESPHome
          </h2>
        </div>


        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-10 lg:gap-16 items-center">

          <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)]">
            <img
              src={`${base}/projects/home-assistant/esp32.png`}
              alt="Custom ESP32 Home Assistant controller"
              class="w-full object-cover"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>


          <div class="text-[var(--white-icon)] text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-6">

            <p>
              Custom ESP32 devices extend the system beyond commercially
              available smart-home products.
            </p>

            <p>
              ESPHome provides the interface between custom hardware and
              Home Assistant, allowing sensors, controls and displays to
              become native entities inside the smart-home system.
            </p>

            <p>
              This approach also makes it possible to build interfaces and
              devices specifically around the needs of the home. For example, 
              a panel in my bedroom for all the devices that are there instead 
              of going through the entire main dashboard just to control that rooms 
              devices.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- Engineering challenges -->
      <section class="mt-8">

        <div class="mb-10">
          <p class="text-base md:text-lg uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)] mb-4 shiny-sec">
            Engineering Challenges
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-medium leading-tight">
            Problems worth solving
          </h2>
        </div>


        <div class="space-y-5">

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] p-7">
            <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">
              Keeping the system local
            </h3>

            <p class="text-[var(--white-icon)] leading-relaxed">
              Designed the system around local communication wherever
              practical, reducing reliance on cloud services and external
              dependencies.
            </p>
          </div>


          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] p-7">
            <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">
              Integrating different ecosystems
            </h3>

            <p class="text-[var(--white-icon)] leading-relaxed">
              Combined devices using different protocols and integrations
              into a unified Home Assistant environment.
            </p>
          </div>


          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[var(--white-icon-tr)] p-7">
            <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">
              Making automations reliable
            </h3>

            <p class="text-[var(--white-icon)] leading-relaxed">
              Automations need to account for device state, timing,
              presence and changing conditions rather than simply executing
              a single action.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

      </section>


      <!-- Future -->
      <section class="mt-8">

        <div class="mb-10">
          <p class="text-base md:text-lg uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)] mb-4 shiny-sec">
            What's Next
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-medium leading-tight">
            Continuing to evolve the system
          </h2>
        </div>

        <p class="max-w-3xl text-[var(--white-icon)] text-lg leading-relaxed">
          The system is intentionally modular, making it possible to add
          additional sensors, automations, computer-vision capabilities and
          custom hardware over time.
        </p>

      </section>


      <!-- Footer navigation -->
      <section class="mt-28 pt-10 border-t border-[#ffffff10]">

        <a
          href={`${base}/`}
          class="text-[var(--white-icon)] hover:text-white transition"
        >
          ← Back to Portfolio
        </a>

      </section>

    </main>

  </body>
</html>


<style is:global>
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Montserrat Variable";
    src: url("/portfolio/fonts/montserrat-latin-wght-normal.woff2")
      format("woff2-variations");
    font-weight: 100 900;
    font-style: normal;
    font-display: swap;
  }

  :root {
    --background: #101010;
    --sec: #a476ff;
    --white: #dfdfdf;
    --white-icon: #f3f3f398;
    --white-icon-tr: #f3f3f310;
  }

  * {
    font-family:
      "Montserrat Variable",
      -apple-system,
      system-ui,
      sans-serif;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  *::selection {
    background-color: var(--sec);
    color: var(--background);
  }
</style>